== Chapter 14

#align(center)[IN THE CREATION OF THE WORLD, AND ALL THINGS IN IT, THE TRUE GOD DISTINGUISHED BY CERTAIN MARKS FROM FICTITIOUS GODS.]

#block(inset: (x: 1em))[#emph[Beveridge’s summary.] This chapter begins the second part of the First Book, namely the knowledge of man. Some preliminary matters. I. The creation of the world in general (s. 1 and 2). II. The subject of angels (s. 3-13). III. Bad angels, or devils (s. 13-20); and IV. The practical use to be made of the history of the creation (s. 20-22).]

#block(inset: (x: 1em))[#emph[Sections (Beveridge’s summary).]]

#block(inset: (x: 1em))[1\. The bare fact of creation should lead us to acknowledge God; but to keep us from falling away to the fictions of the heathen, God has been pleased to give us a history of the creation. An impious objection: Why was the world not created sooner? The answer to it. The shrewd saying of an old man.]

#block(inset: (x: 1em))[2\. For the same reason the world was created not in an instant but in six days. The order of creation described, showing that Adam was not created until God had, in his infinite goodness, made ample provision for him.]

#block(inset: (x: 1em))[3\. The doctrine of angels explained. 1. That we may learn from them too to acknowledge God. 2. That we may be on our guard against the errors of the worshipers of angels and of the Manichees. Manicheism refuted. The rule of piety.]

#block(inset: (x: 1em))[4\. The angels were created by God. It is unprofitable to inquire at what time and in what order. The idle chatter of the Pseudo-Dionysius.]

#block(inset: (x: 1em))[5\. The nature, offices, and various names of angels.]

#block(inset: (x: 1em))[6\. Angels are the dispensers of the divine kindness toward us.]

#block(inset: (x: 1em))[7\. Angels are a kind of governors over kingdoms and provinces, but especially the guardians of the elect. It is not certain that every believer is in the charge of a single angel. It is enough that all the angels watch over the safety of the Church.]

#block(inset: (x: 1em))[8\. The number and orders of angels are not defined. Why angels are said to have wings.]

#block(inset: (x: 1em))[9\. Angels are ministering spirits and spiritual essences.]

#block(inset: (x: 1em))[10\. The heathen error of putting angels on the throne of God refuted. 1. By passages of Scripture.]

#block(inset: (x: 1em))[11\. The refutation continued. 2. By inferences from other passages. Why God uses the ministry of angels.]

#block(inset: (x: 1em))[12\. The use of what Scripture teaches about the holy angels.]

#block(inset: (x: 1em))[13\. The doctrine of bad angels, or devils, brought under four heads. 1. That we may guard against their schemes and assaults.]

#block(inset: (x: 1em))[14\. That we may be spurred on to the exercises of piety. Why one angel is often spoken of in the singular.]

#block(inset: (x: 1em))[15\. Since the devil is described as the enemy of man, we should wage unceasing war against him.]

#block(inset: (x: 1em))[16\. The devil’s wickedness comes not from creation but from corruption. It is vain and useless to inquire into the manner, time, and character of the angels’ fall.]

#block(inset: (x: 1em))[17\. Though the devil is always opposed to the will of God in will and effort, he can do nothing without God’s permission and consent.]

#block(inset: (x: 1em))[18\. God so overrules wicked spirits as to permit them to test the faithful and to rule over the wicked.]

#block(inset: (x: 1em))[19\. The nature of bad angels. They are spiritual essences endowed with sense and intelligence.]

#block(inset: (x: 1em))[20\. The last part of the chapter, briefly covering the history of creation and showing what it is important for us to know about God.]

#block(inset: (x: 1em))[21\. The special aim of this knowledge is to keep us from overlooking the perfections of God through ingratitude or thoughtlessness. An example of this first kind of knowledge.]

#block(inset: (x: 1em))[22\. Another aim of this knowledge, namely that, seeing how these things were created for our use, we may be stirred up to trust in God, pray to him, and love him.]

1\. #smallcaps[Although] Isaiah justly charges the worshipers of false gods with stupidity for not learning from the foundations of the earth and the circle of the heavens who the true God is (Isa 40:21), yet our minds are so sluggish and dull that it was necessary for the true God to be portrayed more clearly, so that the faithful might not fall away to the fictions of the heathen. The idea that God is the mind of the world, though the most tolerable that philosophers have suggested, is empty and fleeting; and so it was important that we should be given a more intimate knowledge of him, so that we might not wander to and fro in uncertainty. That is why God willed that there should be a history of the creation—a history on which the faith of the Church might lean, seeking no other God than Him whom Moses sets forth as the Creator and Architect of the world. First, that history marks the span of time, so that the faithful can go back through an unbroken succession of years to the first origin of their race and of all things. This knowledge is of the greatest use, not only as an antidote to the monstrous fables that once prevailed in Egypt and in other regions of the world, but also as a means of showing the eternity of God more clearly against the birth of creation, and so moving us to greater wonder. We must not be moved by the profane jeer that it is strange it did not occur to the Deity sooner to create the heavens and the earth, instead of idly letting an infinite stretch of time slip by, though he could have done it many thousands of ages before, while the present world is drawing to its close before it has completed its six thousandth year. It is neither fitting nor lawful for us to inquire why God delayed so long. If the human mind presumed to do so, it would fail a hundred times in the attempt; nor would it be useful for us to know what God, to test the modesty of our faith, has been pleased to conceal on purpose. It was a shrewd answer that a good old man gave when someone impudently asked, in mockery, what God was doing before the world was created: he said that God was making a hell for the inquisitive.#footnote[August. Confess., lib. 11 c. 12. (Beveridge’s note.)] This rebuke, as weighty as it is severe, should curb the wantonness that tickles many and drives them into perverse and harmful speculation.

In short, let us remember that the invisible God, whose wisdom, power, and justice are incomprehensible, is set before us in the history of Moses as in a mirror, in which his living image is reflected. For just as eyes dimmed by age or weakened by any other cause see nothing distinctly without the help of glasses, so (such is our weakness) if Scripture does not direct us in our search for God, we at once become futile in our thinking. Those who now give free rein to their insolence and refuse to take warning will learn too late, by a horrible destruction, how much better it would have been to regard the secret counsels of God with reverence than to belch out blasphemies that pollute the face of heaven. Augustine justly complains that God is insulted whenever a reason higher than his will is demanded.#footnote[Lib. de Gen. contra Manich. 2, 29.] He also wisely reminds us elsewhere that it is just as wrong to raise questions about infinite stretches of time as about infinite space.#footnote[De Civit. Dei, lib. 11 c. 5.] However wide the circuit of the heavens may be, it still has some definite extent. But if anyone were to remonstrate with God that the empty space beyond it is a hundred times greater than creation, would not every pious mind detest such presumption? Those who charge God with idleness for not having pleased them by creating the world countless ages sooner than he did are guilty of the same madness. To indulge their craving, they try to go outside the world, as if the vast circuit of heaven and earth did not contain things numerous and radiant enough to absorb all our senses; as if, in six thousand years, God had not given evidence enough to exercise our minds in ceaseless meditation. Therefore let us willingly stay hedged in by the boundaries within which God has been pleased to confine our persons and, as it were, to enclose our minds, so that they do not lose themselves by wandering without restraint.

2\. For the same purpose Moses relates that the work of creation was completed not in one moment but in six days. By this detail we are drawn away from fictions to the one God, who divided his work into six days so that we might not be reluctant to devote our whole lives to contemplating it. For though our eyes, whichever way they turn, are compelled to look on the works of God, we see how fleeting our attention is, and how quickly pious thoughts, if any arise, vanish away. Here, too, human reason objects to these gradual steps as inconsistent with the power of God, until it is brought under the obedience of faith and learns to welcome the calm rest to which the sanctification of the seventh day invites us. In the very order of things we ought to ponder carefully the fatherly love of God toward the human race: he did not create Adam until he had generously enriched the earth with all good things. If he had placed him on an earth still barren and empty, if he had given life before light, he might have seemed to pay little regard to Adam’s good. But as it is, he has arranged the movements of the sun and stars for man’s use, filled the air, earth, and water with living creatures, and brought forth every kind of fruit in abundance to supply food; and so, taking on the role of a provident and diligent head of a household, he has shown his wonderful goodness toward us. I only touch on these things briefly; but if they are pondered more attentively, they will make it plain that Moses was a sure witness and herald of the one and only Creator. I do not repeat what I have already explained, namely that the passage speaks not only of the bare essence of God, but also sets before us his eternal Wisdom and Spirit, so that we may not dream of any other God than Him who wills to be recognized in that express image.

3\. But before I begin to deal more fully with the nature of man,#footnote[Ch. 15, and Book 2 ch. 1. (Beveridge’s note.)] it will be proper to say something about angels. For although Moses, accommodating himself to the ignorance of ordinary people, mentions in the history of the creation no works of God other than those that meet our eye, yet, since he later brings in angels as the ministers of God, we easily infer that the one whom they serve is their Creator. So, though Moses, speaking in popular language, did not list the angels among the creatures of God at the very beginning, nothing prevents us from setting out clearly and explicitly what Scripture teaches about them elsewhere. For if we desire to know God by his works, we surely cannot overlook so noble and illustrious an example. Moreover, this branch of doctrine is very necessary for refuting many errors. The minds of many are so dazzled by the excellence of the angels’ nature that they would think the angels wronged if they were placed under the authority of the one God and so made subordinate. Hence an imaginary divinity has been ascribed to them. Manes, too, arose with his sect, inventing for himself two principles, God and the devil; he attributed the origin of good things to God, but assigned all evil natures to the devil as their author. If this delusion took hold of our minds, God would be robbed of his glory in the creation of the world. For since nothing belongs more distinctively to God than eternity and αὐτουσία, that is, self-existence, or existence from himself, if I may so speak, do not those who attribute it to the devil in some degree invest him with the honor of divinity? And where is the omnipotence of God, if the devil has the power to carry out whatever he pleases against God’s will and in spite of his opposition? But the only foundation the Manichees have, namely that it would be impious to ascribe the creation of anything evil to a good God, does not in the least go against the orthodox faith. For that faith does not admit that there is anything naturally evil in the whole universe. The depravity and wickedness of man and of the devil alike, and the sins that come from them, are not from nature but from the corruption of nature; nor did anything at all exist at the beginning that did not show something of the divine wisdom and justice. To meet such perverse notions, we must raise our minds higher than our eyes can reach. It was probably for this purpose that the Nicene Creed, in calling God the creator of all things, expressly mentions things invisible. My care, however, must be to keep within the limits that piety prescribes, so that by indulging in speculations higher than is profitable I do not bewilder the reader and lead him away from the simplicity of the faith. And since the Holy Spirit always teaches us what is useful, but either passes over entirely or touches only briefly on matters of little value for edification, it is certainly our duty to remain willingly ignorant of all such matters.

4\. Since angels are the ministers appointed to carry out the commands of God, they must, of course, be admitted to be his creatures; but to stir up questions about the time or order in which they were created#footnote[See Lombard, lib. 2 dist. 2, sqq. (Beveridge’s note.)] shows more perverseness than diligence. Moses relates that the heavens and the earth were finished, with all their host (Gen 2:1); what is the use of anxiously asking on which day other, more hidden heavenly hosts, besides the stars and planets, also began to exist? Not to dwell on this, let us remember here that in the whole subject of religion one rule of modesty and sobriety is to be kept, and it is this: in obscure matters, not to speak or think, or even long to know, anything more than the Word of God has delivered to us. A second rule is that in reading the Scriptures we should constantly direct our inquiry and meditation to the things that make for edification, and not indulge our curiosity or the study of useless things. And since the Lord has been pleased to instruct us not in frivolous questions but in solid piety, in the fear of his name, in true faith, and in the duties of holiness, let us rest content with that knowledge. So, if we want to be rightly wise, we must leave behind the empty babblings of idle men about the nature, ranks, and number of angels, which have no authority from the Word of God. I know that many seize on these topics more eagerly and take more pleasure in them than in those that bear on daily practice. But if we are not unwilling to be the disciples of Christ, let us not be unwilling to follow the method he has prescribed. In this way, content with him as our master, we will not only refrain from the needless speculations he discourages, but even recoil from them. No one can deny that Dionysius (whoever he may have been) has many shrewd and subtle discussions in his Celestial Hierarchy; but anyone who looks at them more closely will see that they are for the most part nothing but idle talk. The task of a theologian, however, is not to tickle the ear but to strengthen the conscience, by teaching what is true, certain, and useful. If you read that book, you would think the man had come down from heaven and was relating not what he had learned but what he had seen with his own eyes. Yet Paul, though he was caught up to the third heaven (2 Cor 12:1-4), not only told nothing of the kind, but even declared that it was not lawful for a man to speak the secrets he had seen. Saying farewell, then, to that trifling wisdom, let us try to learn from the simple teaching of Scripture what the Lord wants us to know about angels.

5\. In Scripture, then, we uniformly read that angels are heavenly spirits whose obedience and ministry God employs to carry out all the purposes he has decreed; and that is where their name comes from, since God uses them as a kind of intermediary messengers to make himself known to men. The other names by which they are distinguished also refer to the same office. They are called hosts because they surround their Prince as his court, adorn his majesty and make it visible; like soldiers, they always keep their eyes on their leader’s standard, and are so ready and quick to carry out his orders that the moment he nods, they make ready for the work, or rather are already at it. To declare the magnificence of the divine throne, the prophets give similar pictures, and especially Daniel, when he says that when God rose to give judgment, “thousands upon thousands attended Him, and myriads upon myriads stood before Him” (Dan 7:10). As by these means the Lord wonderfully exerts and declares the power and might of his hand, they are called Virtues. Again, as his government of the world is exercised and administered by them, they are called now Principalities, now Powers, now Dominions (Col 1:16; Eph 1:21). Lastly, as the glory of God in some measure dwells in them, they are also termed Thrones; though about this last name I would not wish to speak positively, since a different interpretation fits equally well, if not better. Leaving aside, then, the name of Thrones, the Holy Spirit often uses the other names to commend the dignity of the angels’ ministry. Nor is it right to pass over without honor those instruments through whom God especially shows the presence of his power. Indeed, they are more than once called gods, because in their ministry, as in a mirror, the Deity is in some measure represented to us. I am quite willing, however, to agree with the ancient writers that in those passages#footnote[Gen 18:2; 32:1, 28; Josh 5:14; Judg 6:14; 13:10, 22.] where we are told that the angel of the Lord appeared to Abraham, Jacob, Moses, and others, Christ was that angel. Still, it is true that when all the angels are spoken of, they are often given this name. Nor ought this to seem strange. For if princes and rulers are given this honor (Ps 82:6) because in their office they stand in the place of God, the supreme King and Judge, there is far greater reason to give it to angels, in whom the brightness of the divine glory shines out far more fully.

6\. But the point on which Scripture especially insists is the one that tends most to our comfort and to the confirmation of our faith, namely that angels are the ministers and dispensers of the divine kindness toward us. So we are told how they keep watch for our safety, take up our defense, direct our paths, and take care that no evil comes upon us. There are universal declarations that apply first to Christ, the Head of the Church, and after him to all believers. “He will command His angels concerning you to guard you in all your ways. They will lift you up in their hands, so that you will not strike your foot against a stone.” Again, “The angel of the #smallcaps[Lord] encamps around those who fear Him, and he delivers them.”#footnote[Ps 91:11; 34:7; Gen 16:9; 24:7; 48:16; Exod 14:19; 23:20; Judg 2:1, 20; 6:11; 13:10; Matt 4:11; Luke 22:43; Matt 28:5; Luke 24:5; Acts 1:10; 2 Kgs 19:35; Isa 37:36.] By these passages the Lord shows that he has delegated to his angels the protection of those he has undertaken to defend. Accordingly, an angel of the Lord consoles Hagar in her flight and tells her to be reconciled to her mistress. Abraham promises his servant that an angel will guide him on his journey. Jacob, in blessing Ephraim and Manasseh, prays, “The angel who has redeemed me from all harm—may He bless these boys.” So an angel was appointed to guard the camp of the Israelites; and whenever God was pleased to rescue Israel from the hands of its enemies, he raised up avengers through the ministry of angels. Finally (to mention no more), angels ministered to Christ and stood by him in all his distresses. To the women they announced his resurrection; to the disciples they foretold his glorious coming. In discharging the office of our protectors, they war against the devil and all our enemies, and carry out the vengeance of God on those who afflict us. So we read that an angel of the Lord, to deliver Jerusalem from siege, struck down one hundred and eighty-five thousand men in the camp of the king of Assyria in a single night.

7\. Whether each believer has a single angel assigned to defend him, I dare not say for certain. When Daniel brings in the angel of the Persians and the angel of the Greeks, he certainly indicates that particular angels are appointed as a kind of governors over kingdoms and provinces.#footnote[Dan 10:13, 20; 12:1; Matt 18:10; Luke 15:7; 16:22; 2 Kgs 6:17; Acts 12:15.] Again, when Christ says that the angels of children always see the face of his Father, he implies that there are particular angels to whom their safety has been entrusted. But I do not know whether it can be inferred from this that each believer has his own angel. This much I hold as certain: that each of us is cared for not by one angel only, but that all the angels with one accord keep watch for our safety. For it is said of all the angels together that they rejoice more “over one sinner who repents than over ninety-nine righteous ones who do not need to repent.” It is also said that angels (more than one) carried the soul of Lazarus into Abraham’s bosom. Nor was it in vain that Elisha showed his servant the many chariots of fire that were set apart especially for him.

There is one passage that seems to indicate somewhat more clearly that each individual has his own angel. When Peter, after his release from prison, knocked at the door of the house where the brethren were gathered, they could not believe it was Peter himself, and said it was his angel. This idea seems to have come to them from a common belief that every believer has a single angel assigned to him. Here, however, it may be answered that nothing prevents us from taking it of any one of the angels to whom the Lord might have given charge of Peter at that particular time, without implying that this angel was to be his permanent guardian, as ordinary people imagine#footnote[See Calvin on Mark 5:9. (Beveridge’s note.)] that two angels, a good one and a bad one, are assigned to each person as attendant spirits of a kind. After all, it is not worth anxiously investigating a point that does not greatly concern us. If anyone does not think it enough to know that all the ranks of the heavenly host are always keeping watch for his safety, I do not see what he could gain by knowing that he has one angel as his special guardian. Those, again, who limit to a single angel the care God takes of each of us do great wrong to themselves and to all the members of the Church, as if there were no value in those promised reinforcements which surround and defend us on every side and embolden us to fight more bravely.

8\. Those who presume to lay down the law on the ranks and numbers of angels would do well to consider what foundation they stand on. As to rank, I admit that Michael is described by Daniel as a mighty Prince, and by Jude as an Archangel.#footnote[Dan 12:1; Jude 9; 1 Thess 4:16; Dan 10:13, 21; Luke 1:19, 26; Tobit 3:17; 5:5; Matt 26:53; Dan 7:10; 2 Kgs 6:17; Ps 34:7.] Paul also tells us that an archangel will sound the trumpet that is to summon the world to judgment. But how can anyone determine from such passages the grades of honor among the angels, mark out the insignia of each, and assign each his place and station? Even the two names found in Scripture, Michael and Gabriel, or a third if you want to add one from the story of Tobit, seem from their meaning to have been given to angels to suit the weakness of our understanding, though I would rather not speak positively on the point. As to the number of angels, we learn from the mouth of our Savior that there are many legions, and from Daniel that there are many myriads. Elisha’s servant saw a multitude of chariots; and their vast number is shown by the statement that they encamp around those who fear the Lord. It is certain that spirits have no bodily form; and yet Scripture, accommodating itself to us, pictures them as winged Cherubim and Seraphim. This is not without cause: it assures us that when the need arises they will hurry to our aid with incredible swiftness, flying to us with the speed of lightning. Beyond this, as to both the ranks and the numbers of angels, let us count them among those mysteries whose full revelation is put off until the last day, and so keep ourselves from overcurious inquiry and presumptuous talk.

9\. There is one point, however, that some restless men call into question, but that we ought to hold as certain, namely that angels are ministering spirits (Heb 1:14), whose service God uses for the protection of his people, and through whom he both distributes his favors among men and carries out his other works. The Sadducees of old held that by angels nothing more was meant than the impulses God stirs up in men, or the displays he gives of his own power (Acts 23:8). But this fantasy is contradicted by so many passages of Scripture that it seems strange such gross ignorance could have found any acceptance among the Jews. To say nothing of the passages I have already quoted—passages that refer to thousands and legions of angels, speak of them as rejoicing, as bearing up the faithful in their hands, carrying their souls to rest, seeing the face of their Father, and so on#footnote[Luke 15:10; Ps 91:11; Matt 4:6; Luke 4:10; 16:22; Matt 18:10; Acts 7:53; Gal 3:19; Matt 22:30; 24:36; 25:31; Luke 9:26; 1 Tim 5:21; Heb 1:4; 2:16; 12:22; Eph 3:10; 1 Pet 1:12; Heb 1:6; Ps 97:7.]—there are other passages that most clearly prove that they are real beings possessed of a spiritual essence. Stephen and Paul say that the Law was given by the hands of angels. Our Savior, moreover, says that at the resurrection the elect will be like angels; that the day of judgment is not known even to the angels; and that he himself will then come with the holy angels. However much such passages may be twisted, their meaning is plain. In the same way, when Paul solemnly charges Timothy to keep his instructions, as before Christ and his elect angels, it is not qualities or inspirations without substance that he means, but true spirits. And when we read in the Epistle to the Hebrews that Christ was made more excellent than the angels, that the world was not made subject to them, and that Christ took on not their nature but man’s, these passages can have no meaning unless we understand that angels are blessed spirits, to whom such comparisons can properly apply. The author of that Epistle declares the same thing when he places the souls of believers and the holy angels together in the kingdom of heaven. Moreover, in the passages already quoted, the angels of children are said to see the face of God; angels are said to defend us by their protection, to rejoice in our salvation, to marvel at the manifold grace of God in the Church, and to be under Christ their head. To the same effect are their frequent appearances to the holy patriarchs in human form, their speaking, and their accepting hospitality. Christ too, because of the preeminence he holds as Mediator, is called the Angel (Mal 3:1). I thought it right to touch on this subject in passing, to put simple people on their guard against the foolish and absurd notions that Satan stirred up many centuries ago and that keep springing up again.

10\. It remains to give warning against the superstition that usually begins to creep in when it is said that all blessings are ministered and dispensed to us by angels. For the human mind is quick to think that there is no honor they ought not to receive; and so the offices that belong to Christ and God alone are bestowed on them. In this way the glory of Christ was greatly obscured for several centuries past, while extravagant praises were heaped on angels without any authority from Scripture. Of the corruptions we oppose today, there is scarcely one older than this. Even Paul appears to have had a severe contest with some who so exalted angels that they almost brought Christ down to the angels’ rank. Hence he so anxiously urges in his Epistle to the Colossians (Col 1:16, 20) that Christ is not only superior to all angels, but that all the endowments they possess are derived from him. In this way he warns us against forsaking Christ by turning to those who are not sufficient for themselves, but must draw with us from a common fountain. Since the radiance of the divine glory shines in them, nothing comes more easily to us than to fall down before them in a kind of stunned adoration, and then to ascribe to them everything that belongs to God alone. Even John confesses in Revelation (Rev 19:10; 22:8, 9) that this happened to him; but he at once adds the answer he was given: “Do not do that! I am a fellow servant with you. Worship God!”

11\. We will happily avoid this danger if we consider why God, instead of acting directly without their agency, is accustomed to employ it in manifesting his power, providing for the safety of his people, and imparting the gifts of his kindness. This he certainly does not do from necessity, as if he were unable to dispense with them. Whenever he pleases, he passes them by and does his own work by a single nod; so far are they from relieving him of any difficulty. So when he uses them, it is as a help to our weakness, so that we may lack nothing that could raise our hopes or strengthen our confidence. It ought, indeed, to be enough for us that the Lord declares himself to be our protector. But when we see ourselves surrounded by so many dangers, so many harms, so many kinds of enemies, such is our frailty and softness that we might at times be filled with alarm or driven to despair, if the Lord did not make his gracious presence known to us in some way suited to our feeble capacity. For this reason he not only promises to take care of us, but assures us that he has countless attendants to whom he has entrusted our safety, and that, whatever dangers may threaten, as long as we are surrounded by their protection and guardianship, we are placed beyond all risk of harm. I admit that, once we have the simple promise that the one God protects us, it is wrong of us still to look around for help. But since the Lord, in his infinite goodness and gentleness, is pleased to provide for this weakness of ours, it would ill become us to neglect the favor. We have an example of this in Elisha’s servant (2 Kgs 6:17). Seeing the mountain surrounded by the army of the Syrians, and no way of escape, he was completely overcome with terror and thought it was all over with himself and his master. Then Elisha prayed God to open his servant’s eyes, and at once the servant saw the mountain filled with horses and chariots of fire—that is, with a multitude of angels, to whose keeping he and the prophet had been entrusted. Strengthened by the vision, he took heart, and could boldly defy the enemy whose appearance had earlier filled him with dismay.

12\. Whatever, therefore, is said about the ministry of angels, let us employ it to remove all distrust and to strengthen our confidence in God. Since the Lord has provided us with such protection, let us not be terrified by the number of our enemies, as if they could prevail in spite of his help, but let us take up Elisha’s words, that there are more for us than against us. How absurd it is, then, to let ourselves be led away from God by angels, who have been appointed for the very purpose of assuring us that his help is all the nearer to us! But we are led away in this way if angels do not lead us straight to him, so that we look to him, call on him, and proclaim him as our only defender; if they are not regarded as his hands, which move to no work except as he directs; if they do not direct us to Christ as the only Mediator, so that we depend wholly on him, lean on him, look to him, and rest in him. What Jacob saw in his vision (Gen 28:12) ought to be fixed deep in our minds: angels coming down to earth to men, and going up again from men to heaven, by a ladder at the top of which the Lord of Hosts was seated. This shows that it is only through the intercession of Christ that the ministry of angels reaches us, as he himself declares: “You#super[+] will see heaven open and the angels of God ascending and descending on the Son of Man” (John 1:51). So Abraham’s servant, though he had been entrusted to the care of an angel (Gen 24:7), does not therefore call on that angel to be with him; but, relying on that assurance, he pours out his prayers before the Lord and asks him to show mercy to Abraham. For just as God does not make angels the ministers of his power and goodness in order to share his glory with them, so he does not promise us his help through their ministry in order that we may divide our confidence between him and them. Away, then, with that Platonic philosophy of seeking access to God through angels and of courting them to make God more favorable to us,#footnote[Plat. in Epinomide et Cratylo.]—a philosophy that presumptuous and superstitious men tried from the first to bring into our religion, and in which they persist even now.

13\. All that Scripture teaches about devils aims at putting us on our guard against their schemes and plots, so that we may arm ourselves with weapons strong enough to drive back the most formidable enemies. For when Satan is called the god and ruler of this world, the strong man armed, the prince of the power of the air, the roaring lion,#footnote[2 Cor 4:4; John 12:31; Matt 12:29; Eph 2:2; 1 Pet 5:8.] the purpose of all these descriptions is to make us more cautious and watchful, and more ready for the fight. This is sometimes said in so many words. For Peter, after describing the devil as a roaring lion prowling around, seeking someone to devour, immediately adds the exhortation, “Resist him, standing firm in your#super[+] faith” (1 Pet 5:9). And Paul, after reminding us that our struggle is not against flesh and blood, but against principalities, against powers, against the rulers of the darkness of this world, against spiritual wickedness in the heavenly places, at once tells us to put on armor equal to so great and dangerous a contest (Eph 6:12). So let this be the use to which we turn all these statements. Forewarned that an enemy is always close at hand—an enemy most daring, most powerful, most cunning, most tireless, most fully supplied with every engine of war, and most expert in the art of fighting—let us not allow ourselves to be overcome by sloth or cowardice. On the contrary, with minds roused and always on the alert, let us stand ready to resist; and, knowing that this warfare ends only with death, let us strive to persevere. Above all, fully aware of our weakness and lack of skill, let us call on God for help and attempt nothing without relying on him, since it is his alone to supply counsel and strength and courage and arms.

14\. That we may feel the more strongly urged to do so, Scripture declares that the enemies who wage war against us are not one or two, or a few, but a great host. Mary Magdalene is said to have been set free from seven devils that possessed her; and our Savior assures us that it commonly happens that when a devil has been cast out, if a way in is opened to it again, it brings seven other spirits more wicked than itself and takes possession again of the empty dwelling. Indeed, one man is said to have been possessed by a whole legion.#footnote[Mark 16:9; Matt 12:43; Luke 8:30.] By this, then, we are taught that the number of enemies with whom we have to war is infinite, so that we do not grow slack in the battle by despising them as few, or give way to sloth by imagining that we are sometimes granted a truce. When one Satan or devil is often spoken of in the singular, what is meant is that dominion of iniquity which is opposed to the reign of righteousness. For as the Church and the communion of saints has Christ for its head, so the faction of the wicked, and wickedness itself, is pictured with its prince ruling supreme over it. Hence the saying, “Depart, you#super[+] who are cursed, into the eternal fire prepared for the devil and his angels” (Matt 25:41).

15\. One thing that ought to spur us on to unceasing combat with the devil is that he is everywhere called both our adversary and the adversary of God. For if the glory of God is dear to us, as it should be, we ought to fight with all our strength against him who aims to extinguish that glory. If we are moved, as we ought to be, by zeal to uphold the kingdom of Christ, we must wage irreconcilable war with him who plots its ruin. Again, if we have any concern for our own salvation, we ought to make neither peace nor truce with him who is continually laying traps to destroy it. And this is how Satan is described in the third chapter of Genesis, where he lures man away from the obedience he owed to God, so that he may both rob God of his due honor and plunge man headlong into ruin. So too he is described in the Gospels (Matt 13:28), where he is called the enemy and is said to sow weeds in order to corrupt the seed of eternal life. In short, in all his deeds we find true what our Savior says of him, that he was a murderer and a liar from the beginning (John 8:44). He attacks the truth with lies and clouds the light with darkness. He entangles the minds of men in error; he stirs up hatred and kindles strife and war—all in order to overthrow the kingdom of God and drown men in eternal perdition with himself. From this it is clear that his whole nature is depraved, spiteful, and malicious. There must be extreme depravity in a mind bent on attacking the glory of God and the salvation of man. John indicates this in his Epistle when he says that the devil “has been sinning from the very start” (1 John 3:8), meaning that he is the author, leader, and architect of all malice and wickedness.

16\. But since the devil was created by God, we must remember that the malice we attribute to his nature comes not from creation but from corruption. Everything damnable in him he brought on himself by his revolt and fall. Scripture reminds us of this so that we may not believe he was created so at first, and ascribe to God what is utterly foreign to God’s nature. For this reason Christ declares (John 8:44) that Satan, when he lies, “speaks from what is his own,” and gives the reason: “because he did not stand in the truth.” By saying that he did not stand in the truth, Christ certainly implies that Satan was once in the truth; and by calling him the father of lies, he leaves Satan no ground for charging God with the depravity of which he himself was the cause. And although these words are brief and not very explicit, they are fully enough to clear the majesty of God of every slander. And what does it matter to us to know more about devils, or to know it for any other end? Some grumble because Scripture does not, in various passages, give a clear and orderly account of Satan’s fall, its cause, manner, time, and nature. But since these things do not concern us, it was better, if not to pass them over in complete silence, at least to touch on them only lightly. The Holy Spirit would not stoop to feeding curiosity with idle, unprofitable stories. We see that it was the Lord’s purpose to deliver nothing in his sacred oracles that we might not learn for our edification. So, instead of dwelling on needless matters, let it be enough for us to hold briefly, about the nature of devils, that at their first creation they were angels of God, but by their revolt they both ruined themselves and became instruments of perdition to others. Because it was useful to know this much, Peter and Jude teach it clearly: “God,” they say, “did not spare the angels who sinned, who did not keep their first estate but left their own dwelling” (2 Pet 2:4; Jude 6). And Paul, by speaking of the elect angels (1 Tim 5:21), clearly draws an unspoken contrast between them and reprobate angels.

17\. As to the strife and war that Satan is said to wage against God, it must be understood with this qualification: Satan can do nothing at all without the will and consent of God. For we read in the history of Job that Satan presents himself before God to receive his orders, and does not dare to set about any undertaking until he has been given leave. In the same way, when Ahab was to be deceived, Satan undertook to be a lying spirit in the mouth of all the prophets, and when the Lord sent him, he carried it out. For this reason, too, the spirit that tormented Saul is called an evil spirit from the Lord, because it was, as it were, the scourge with which the wicked king’s sins were punished. Elsewhere it is said that the plagues of Egypt were inflicted by God through wicked angels. In line with these particular examples, Paul declares in general that unbelievers are blinded by God, though he had earlier described this as the work of Satan.#footnote[Job 1:6; 2:1; 1 Kgs 22:20; 1 Sam 16:14; 18:10; Ps 78:49; 2 Thess 2:9, 11.] It is clear, then, that Satan is under the power of God, and is so ruled by his authority that he must obey it. Moreover, when we say that Satan resists God and that his works are at odds with God’s works, we maintain at the same time that this opposition and conflict depend on the permission of God. I am not speaking now of Satan’s will and effort, but only of the outcome. For since the devil is wicked in disposition, he has no inclination at all to obey the divine will, but on the contrary is wholly bent on defiance and rebellion. This much, then, he has from himself and his own iniquity: that he opposes God eagerly and deliberately, aiming at the things he thinks most contrary to the will of God. But since God holds him bound and fettered by the curb of his power, Satan carries out only those things for which permission has been given him; and so, willing or not, he obeys his Creator, being forced to serve Him wherever He drives him.

18\. God, turning the unclean spirits this way and that as he pleases, uses them to exercise believers: they fight against them, attack them with schemes, ply them with temptations, press hard on them, often wear them out, trouble them, alarm them, and sometimes wound them, but never conquer or overwhelm them. The wicked, on the other hand, they hold in bondage; they rule over their minds and bodies and use them as slaves for every kind of wickedness. Because believers are troubled by such enemies, they are given exhortations like these: “Do not give the devil a foothold”; “Your#super[+] adversary the devil prowls around like a roaring lion, seeking someone to devour. Resist him, standing firm in your#super[+] faith” (Eph 4:27; 1 Pet 5:8). Paul admits that he was not exempt from this kind of conflict when he says that, to subdue his pride, a messenger of Satan was sent to torment him (2 Cor 12:7). This trial, then, is common to all the children of God. But since the promise of crushing Satan’s head (Gen 3:15) applies to Christ and to all his members alike, I deny that believers can ever be overwhelmed or conquered by him. They are often thrown into alarm, it is true, but never so completely that they do not recover. They fall under the force of the blows, but they get up again; they are wounded, but not mortally. In short, they toil on through the whole course of their lives in such a way that in the end they gain the victory. This, however, I do not apply to every single act. For we know how David, through the just anger of God, was given over to Satan for a time, and at Satan’s prompting numbered the people (2 Sam 24:1); and it is not without reason that Paul holds out a hope of pardon to any who may have been caught in the snares of the devil (2 Tim 2:26). So elsewhere Paul shows that the promise quoted above begins in this life, where the struggle goes on, and is fulfilled after the struggle is over. His words are: “The God of peace will soon crush Satan under your#super[+] feet” (Rom 16:20). In our Head, indeed, this victory was always complete, because the prince of the world “has no claim” on him (John 14:30); but in us, who are his members, it is now won in part, and will be made complete when we have put off our mortal flesh, through which we are still subject to weakness, and have been filled with the power of the Holy Spirit. In this way, when the kingdom of Christ is raised up and established, Satan’s kingdom falls, as our Lord himself puts it: “I saw Satan fall like lightning from heaven” (Luke 10:18). With these words he confirmed the report the apostles gave of the efficacy of their preaching. Likewise he says, “When a strong man, fully armed, guards his house, his possessions are secure. But when someone stronger attacks and overpowers him, he takes away the armor in which the man trusted, and then he divides up his plunder” (Luke 11:21, 22). And to this end Christ, by dying, conquered Satan, who had the power of death (Heb 2:14), and triumphed over all his hosts, so that they might not harm the Church, which otherwise Satan would overthrow a hundred times every moment. For (such is our weakness, and such his raging fury) how could we hold out for any time at all, however short, against his many and unceasing assaults, if we did not rely on the victory of our leader? God, then, does not allow Satan to rule over the souls of believers, but gives over to his rule only the ungodly and unbelieving, whom he does not see fit to count among his flock. For the devil is said to hold undisputed possession of this world until he is driven out by Christ. Likewise he is said to blind all who do not believe the Gospel, and to do his own work in the children of disobedience. And rightly so; for all the wicked are vessels of wrath. To whom, then, should they be subjected but to the minister of the divine vengeance? Finally, they are said to be of their father the devil.#footnote[John 12:31; 2 Cor 4:4; Eph 2:2; Rom 9:22; John 8:44; 1 John 3:8.] For as believers are recognized as the sons of God because they bear his image, so the wicked are rightly regarded as the children of Satan, because they have degenerated into his image.

19\. Just as we refuted above that trifling philosophy about the holy angels which teaches that they are nothing but good impulses or inspirations that God stirs up in the minds of men, so here too we must refute those who foolishly claim that devils are nothing but evil passions or disturbances suggested by our carnal nature. A brief refutation is found in passages of Scripture on this subject, passages neither few nor obscure. First, when they are called unclean spirits and apostate angels (Matt 12:43; Jude, verse 6), who have fallen away from their origin, the very names show clearly enough that they are not impulses or feelings of the mind, but truly, as they are called, minds or spirits endowed with sense and understanding. In the same way, when the children of God are contrasted by John, and also by our Savior, with the children of the devil (John 8:44; 1 John 3:10), would the contrast not be absurd if the word devil meant nothing more than evil inspirations? And John adds still more emphatically that the devil has been sinning from the very start (1 John 3:8). In the same way, when Jude brings in the archangel Michael contending with the devil (Jude, verse 9), he is certainly setting a wicked and rebellious angel against a good one. With this agrees the account in the Book of Job (Job 1:6; 2:1) that Satan appeared before God with the holy angels. But the clearest passages of all are those that mention the punishment which the devils, by the judgment of God, already begin to feel, and will feel above all at the resurrection: “What do You want with us, Son of God? Have You come here to torture us before the appointed time?” (Matt 8:29); and again, “Depart, you#super[+] who are cursed, into the eternal fire prepared for the devil and his angels” (Matt 25:41). Again, “If God did not spare the angels who sinned, but cast them deep into hell, placing them in chains of darkness to be held for judgment,” and so on (2 Pet 2:4). How absurd it would be to say that devils are doomed to eternal punishment, that fire is prepared for them, that they are even now tortured and tormented by the glory of Christ, if there were really no devils at all! But since this subject needs no argument for those who believe the Word of God, while little is gained by quoting Scripture to those empty speculators whom nothing but novelty can please, I think I have already done enough for my purpose, which was to put the godly on their guard against the wild delusions with which restless men trouble themselves and simpler people. The subject, however, was worth touching on, so that none who are caught in that error should imagine they have no enemy, and so become slacker or less careful in resisting.

20\. Meanwhile, placed as we are in this most beautiful theater, let us not be unwilling to take a pious delight in the clear and evident works of God. For, as we have said elsewhere, though it is not the chief lesson of faith, it is, in the order of things, the first: to remember, whichever way we turn, that all that meets the eye is the work of God, and at the same time to reflect with pious care on the end for which God created it. So, in order to grasp with true faith what we need to know about God, it is important to pay attention to the history of the creation, as Moses briefly recorded it and as pious writers, especially Basil and Ambrose, afterwards set it out more fully. From this history we learn that God, by the power of his Word and his Spirit, created the heavens and the earth out of nothing; that he then brought forth lifeless and living things of every kind, setting an innumerable variety of things in wonderful order, and giving each kind its proper nature, office, place, and station; and that, since all things were subject to decay, he provided for the preservation of each single species until the last day. Some he nurtures in secret ways and, as it were, from time to time breathes new vigor into them; on others he has bestowed the power of continuing their kind, so that it does not perish when they die. When heaven and earth had thus been most richly adorned and abundantly supplied with all things, like a large and splendid mansion, magnificently built and exquisitely furnished, at last man was made—man, by the beauty of his person and his many noble gifts, the most glorious example of the works of God. But since I do not intend to tell the history of the creation in detail, it is enough to have touched on it again briefly in passing. I have already reminded my reader that the best course for him is to draw his knowledge of the subject from Moses and others who have carefully and faithfully handed down an account of the creation.

21\. It is unnecessary to dwell at length on the end that should be aimed at in considering the works of God. The subject has to a great extent been explained elsewhere, and, as far as our present work requires, it may now be disposed of in a few words. Undoubtedly, if one were to attempt to speak in fitting terms of the inestimable wisdom, power, justice, and goodness of God in the forming of the world, no grace or splendor of diction could equal the greatness of the subject. Still, there is no doubt that the Lord wants us to be constantly occupied with such holy meditation, so that, as we contemplate the immense treasures of wisdom, justice, goodness, and power displayed in all created things as in so many mirrors, we should not merely run our eyes over them with a hasty and, as it were, fleeting glance, but dwell on them long, turn them over in our minds seriously and faithfully, and call them to mind again and again. But as the present work is of a didactic nature, we cannot fittingly enter on topics that require lengthy discourse. Therefore, to be brief, let the reader understand that he has grasped by true faith what it means that God is the Creator of heaven and earth, first, if he follows the general rule never to pass over ungratefully, thoughtlessly, or forgetfully the glorious perfections that God displays in the things he has made; and secondly, if he applies what he sees to himself, so as to fix it deeply in his heart. The first is exemplified when we consider how great the Architect must be who framed and ordered the multitude of the starry host so admirably that no more glorious sight can be imagined: setting some in their stations and fixing them there so that they cannot move; giving others a freer course, yet setting limits to their wandering; so regulating the motion of the whole as to measure out day and night, months, years, and seasons; and at the same time so adjusting the unequal length of the days that there is no confusion at all. The first course is also exemplified when we observe his power in upholding so vast a mass and in guiding the swift revolutions of the heavenly bodies, and so on. These few examples sufficiently explain what is meant by recognizing the divine perfections in the creation of the world. Were we to attempt to go over the whole subject, we would never come to a conclusion; for there are as many miracles of divine power, as many striking evidences of wisdom and goodness, as there are kinds of things in the universe—indeed, as there are individual things, great or small.

22\. There remains the other course, which comes closer to faith: that while we observe how God has appointed all things for our good and salvation, we at the same time feel his power and grace both in ourselves and in the great blessings he has bestowed on us, and so stir ourselves up to trust in him, to call on him, to praise him, and to love him. Moreover, as I noted a little earlier, the Lord himself, by the very order of creation, has shown that he created all things for man’s sake. For it was not without reason that he divided the making of the world into six days, though it would have been no harder for him to complete the whole work, in all its parts, in one moment than by gradual steps. But he was pleased to show his providence and fatherly care toward us in this: before he formed man, he provided everything he foresaw would be useful and beneficial to him. How ungrateful it would be, then, to doubt whether we are cared for by this best of Fathers, who we see cared for us even before we were born! How impious it would be to tremble with distrust, fearing that we might one day be abandoned in our need by that kindness which, before we existed, showed itself in a complete supply of all good things! Moreover, Moses tells us (Gen 1:28; 9:2) that everything the world contains has been generously placed at our disposal. God certainly did not do this to mock us with an empty deed of gift. So nothing that concerns our safety will ever be lacking to us. To conclude in one word: whenever we call God the Creator of heaven and earth, let us remember that the disposal of all the things he has created is in his hand and power, and that we are his sons, whom he has received into his charge and keeping, to nourish and bring up. This is so that we may expect the sum of all good from him alone, and have full hope that he will never allow us to lack the things necessary to salvation, so as to leave us depending on some other source; so that in everything we desire we may address our prayers to him, and in every benefit we receive acknowledge his hand and give him thanks; and so that, drawn by his great goodness and kindness, we may strive with our whole heart to love and serve him.
